1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,427
Under a small town in central Turkey, there is a second town.

2
00:00:04,427 --> 00:00:15,166
It goes down about eighty-five metres, through soft volcanic rock, and it was made to hold as many as twenty thousand people, with their animals and their food.

3
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:18,014
This is Derinkuyu, in Cappadocia.

4
00:00:18,014 --> 00:00:19,818
Its name means deep well.

5
00:00:19,818 --> 00:00:24,292
And everyone who walks down its stairs asks the same question.

6
00:00:24,292 --> 00:00:28,333
Who would dig all this, and what were they so afraid of?

7
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:31,684
Online, the answers get bigger every year.

8
00:00:31,684 --> 00:00:34,638
They were hiding from attacks from the sky.

9
00:00:34,638 --> 00:00:35,599
From visitors.

10
00:00:35,599 --> 00:00:40,133
From a comet that set the world on fire twelve thousand years ago.

11
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:42,812
So let's do something different.

12
00:00:42,812 --> 00:00:49,588
Let's live through one day of a siege, hour by hour, the way the people of this city actually did.

13
00:00:49,588 --> 00:00:52,700
A reconstructed day, built from real records.

14
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:58,654
At every hour, we open one part of the city and test the claim that belongs there.

15
00:00:58,654 --> 00:01:01,933
And at dawn tomorrow, a scorecard, and a verdict.

16
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:07,765
Six in the morning.

17
00:01:07,765 --> 00:01:09,850
A rider comes in at a gallop.

18
00:01:09,850 --> 00:01:11,719
Dust on the southern road.

19
00:01:11,719 --> 00:01:13,661
Horsemen, hundreds of them.

20
00:01:13,661 --> 00:01:19,699
For a Byzantine village in Cappadocia, this is the most ordinary nightmare there is.

21
00:01:20,166 --> 00:01:22,525
This land sat on a crossroads.

22
00:01:22,525 --> 00:01:26,220
Trade routes ran through it, and so did armies.

23
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:31,724
Hittites, Phrygians, Persians, Romans, Arab raiders, Seljuks, Mongols.

24
00:01:31,724 --> 00:01:33,453
Empires came and went.

25
00:01:33,453 --> 00:01:34,633
Farmers stayed.

26
00:01:35,099 --> 00:01:43,680
From roughly seven eighty to eleven eighty, during the Arab and Byzantine wars, raids swept through almost every season.

27
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:52,833
UNESCO says that, to resist those invasions, local communities banded together in underground towns like Kaymakli and Derinkuyu.

28
00:01:53,299 --> 00:02:05,866
In the tenth century, the historian Leo the Deacon wrote that the people here were once called troglodytes, because they went underground, into holes and labyrinths, like dens and burrows.

29
00:02:06,333 --> 00:02:07,755
Now the first claim.

30
00:02:07,755 --> 00:02:12,806
In nineteen eighty-two, the writer Erich von Daniken visited Derinkuyu.

31
00:02:12,806 --> 00:02:19,278
His reasoning: a refuge this elaborate must have been built against an attack from the air.

32
00:02:19,278 --> 00:02:23,333
And from there, a step toward visitors from beyond Earth.

33
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,531
But look at what this alarm actually is.

34
00:02:26,531 --> 00:02:27,009
Riders.

35
00:02:27,009 --> 00:02:31,857
Men on foot and on horseback, who can only reach you through a doorway.

36
00:02:31,857 --> 00:02:36,363
Underground is a terrible place to hide from something that flies.

37
00:02:36,363 --> 00:02:39,299
It's a perfect place to hide from soldiers.

38
00:02:39,766 --> 00:02:43,900
So at six o'clock, the village does what its grandparents did.

39
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:44,833
It disappears.

40
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:46,245
Seven o'clock.

41
00:02:46,245 --> 00:02:48,610
Where do twenty thousand people go?

42
00:02:48,610 --> 00:02:50,434
Not through one grand gate.

43
00:02:50,434 --> 00:02:58,000
More than six hundred entrances have been found, many hidden in the basements and courtyards of ordinary houses.

44
00:02:58,466 --> 00:03:00,975
That's how the modern world found it.

45
00:03:00,975 --> 00:03:10,265
In nineteen sixty-three, a local man renovating his house knocked through a wall and found a room behind it, then a passage, then a city.

46
00:03:10,265 --> 00:03:14,266
Guides add that his chickens kept vanishing into the crack.

47
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:17,195
And this is why it was possible.

48
00:03:17,195 --> 00:03:23,811
Cappadocia is built of tuff, volcanic ash from ancient eruptions, compacted into rock.

49
00:03:23,811 --> 00:03:29,966
The local Greek name for the city, Malakopia, probably comes from malakos: soft.

50
00:03:30,433 --> 00:03:42,017
Classicist Andrea De Giorgi explains that the dry ground and soft rock made the region ideal for digging, with simple tools like shovels and pickaxes.

51
00:03:42,017 --> 00:03:43,948
Freshly cut tuff is soft.

52
00:03:43,948 --> 00:03:46,033
Exposed to air, it hardens.

53
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:49,787
This is not a city that was built in a year.

54
00:03:49,787 --> 00:03:57,782
It was dug, extended, abandoned and dug again, generation after generation, for well over a thousand years.

55
00:03:57,782 --> 00:03:59,800
Every family adding a room.

56
00:04:00,266 --> 00:04:05,160
Some videos claim the tunnels are too smooth, too regular for hand tools.

57
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:12,466
Walk them, and you'll see the opposite: uneven walls, low ceilings, pick marks, soot from centuries of lamps.

58
00:04:12,933 --> 00:04:16,626
By half past seven, the last family is underground.

59
00:04:16,626 --> 00:04:19,233
Now the city has to close its doors.

60
00:04:19,699 --> 00:04:20,669
Eight o'clock.

61
00:04:20,669 --> 00:04:21,777
The stone doors.

62
00:04:21,777 --> 00:04:30,366
At choke points between levels, the builders carved huge round stones, like millstones, sitting in slots beside the passage.

63
00:04:30,833 --> 00:04:34,957
Each one weighs a few hundred kilograms, up to half a tonne.

64
00:04:34,957 --> 00:04:37,363
Rolled across, it seals the tunnel.

65
00:04:37,363 --> 00:04:41,899
And here's the key detail: they can only be moved from the inside.

66
00:04:42,366 --> 00:04:44,500
Many have a small hole in the middle.

67
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:47,500
Defenders could look out, and fight back through it.

68
00:04:47,966 --> 00:04:54,714
A Cambridge linguist, Richard Dawkins, not the biologist, recorded a story from the fourteenth century.

69
00:04:54,714 --> 00:05:05,000
When Timur's armies came, one of his captains was hunting villagers hidden underground, and was killed by an arrow shot through the hole in one of the doors.

70
00:05:05,466 --> 00:05:06,886
Then the corridors.

71
00:05:06,886 --> 00:05:12,343
Low and narrow, so anyone coming in has to walk bent over, one at a time.

72
00:05:12,343 --> 00:05:22,733
Speleologist Roberto Bixio describes a system where whole sections could be sealed off, and counter-attacks launched through side passages.

73
00:05:23,199 --> 00:05:26,320
So ask the doors what they were built against.

74
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:28,152
Not something from the sky.

75
00:05:28,152 --> 00:05:34,733
A stone that only opens from inside, in a tunnel you must crawl through, is built against people.

76
00:05:35,199 --> 00:05:37,761
By nine o'clock, the city is sealed.

77
00:05:37,761 --> 00:05:42,813
Thousands of people, hundreds of animals, a few hundred metres of rock.

78
00:05:42,813 --> 00:05:44,948
Which raises the next problem.

79
00:05:44,948 --> 00:05:45,233
Air.

80
00:05:45,699 --> 00:05:46,580
Ten o'clock.

81
00:05:46,580 --> 00:05:50,104
Lamps burning, cooking fires, animals breathing.

82
00:05:50,104 --> 00:05:53,995
Without fresh air, a sealed city suffocates in hours.

83
00:05:53,995 --> 00:05:58,400
Derinkuyu solves it with more than fifty ventilation shafts.

84
00:05:58,866 --> 00:06:02,391
The main shaft is about three metres by nearly three.

85
00:06:02,391 --> 00:06:09,640
And engineers noticed something clever: the openings to different levels never sit directly above each other.

86
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:12,766
The layout appears designed to keep air moving.

87
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:26,457
Rock engineers Omer Aydan and Resat Ulusay have studied the city since nineteen ninety-six, monitoring temperature, moisture and air pressure, with sensors on the fifth and seventh floors.

88
00:06:26,457 --> 00:06:30,466
Their verdict: a remarkably stable underground structure.

89
00:06:30,933 --> 00:06:33,064
The physics is simple and old.

90
00:06:33,064 --> 00:06:35,266
Warm air rises, cool air sinks.

91
00:06:35,266 --> 00:06:41,090
Underground, the temperature stays at around thirteen to fifteen degrees all year.

92
00:06:41,090 --> 00:06:46,133
Shafts linking levels at different heights keep a slow current flowing.

93
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:48,953
Is that too advanced for farmers?

94
00:06:48,953 --> 00:06:55,941
Every mine, every well-digger, every chimney builder in the ancient world used the same principle.

95
00:06:55,941 --> 00:06:56,797
Clever, yes.

96
00:06:56,797 --> 00:06:57,866
Mysterious, no.

97
00:06:58,333 --> 00:07:00,428
And notice the design choice.

98
00:07:00,428 --> 00:07:07,003
Not one big air hole an enemy could block, but dozens of small ones spread across the city.

99
00:07:07,003 --> 00:07:10,833
Air from fifty shafts is hard to cut off all at once.

100
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:12,284
Noon is coming.

101
00:07:12,284 --> 00:07:13,466
The air is moving.

102
00:07:13,466 --> 00:07:17,733
But twenty thousand people need something else, every single day.

103
00:07:18,199 --> 00:07:19,336
Twelve o'clock.

104
00:07:19,336 --> 00:07:19,791
Water.

105
00:07:19,791 --> 00:07:23,959
The town's name, Derinkuyu, deep well, comes from this.

106
00:07:23,959 --> 00:07:28,733
A shaft about fifty-five metres deep, reaching water far below.

107
00:07:29,199 --> 00:07:34,294
It served the village above in normal times and the people below in a siege.

108
00:07:34,294 --> 00:07:37,109
Crucially, it could be cut off from below.

109
00:07:37,109 --> 00:07:41,466
Raiders at the surface couldn't poison it, or use it as a way in.

110
00:07:41,933 --> 00:07:55,399
Graham Hancock, in the Netflix series Ancient Apocalypse, reads it differently: internal wells, sealed doors, deep air, all planned for a long, total escape from something poisoning the world above.

111
00:07:55,866 --> 00:07:59,848
But a well safe from poison is exactly what a siege needs.

112
00:07:59,848 --> 00:08:03,212
Poisoning or blocking water was standard warfare.

113
00:08:03,212 --> 00:08:07,399
You don't need a comet to want a well your enemy can't reach.

114
00:08:07,866 --> 00:08:12,569
And a fair question for the defenders of the twenty thousand figure.

115
00:08:12,569 --> 00:08:18,378
One well, twenty thousand people, three litres each, is sixty thousand litres a day.

116
00:08:18,378 --> 00:08:21,766
That number is a capacity estimate, not a census.

117
00:08:22,233 --> 00:08:25,136
Most raids lasted days or weeks, not years.

118
00:08:25,136 --> 00:08:27,566
That's what this water had to cover.

119
00:08:28,033 --> 00:08:29,547
Three in the afternoon.

120
00:08:29,547 --> 00:08:30,337
The animals.

121
00:08:30,337 --> 00:08:38,700
Stables sat on the upper levels, near the entrances, where the air was best and the smell stayed away from the living quarters.

122
00:08:39,166 --> 00:08:49,466
Deeper down, kitchens with tandoor ovens, the same kind still used in Cappadocian villages, under ceilings blackened by centuries of smoke.

123
00:08:49,933 --> 00:08:57,241
There are wine presses and oil presses, cellars, storerooms, and long refectories with stone tables.

124
00:08:57,241 --> 00:09:03,233
De Giorgi points to cellars, pressing vats and amphoras as evidence of winemaking.

125
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:07,056
This is not an emergency bunker for a few nights.

126
00:09:07,056 --> 00:09:09,728
It's a village, folded into the ground.

127
00:09:09,728 --> 00:09:12,399
Ready to keep families alive for weeks.

128
00:09:12,866 --> 00:09:13,792
And it's old.

129
00:09:13,792 --> 00:09:19,130
Around four hundred BC, the Greek soldier Xenophon marched across Anatolia.

130
00:09:19,130 --> 00:09:30,733
In the highlands of Armenia, east of here, he saw houses underground, with a mouth like a well, ramps for animals, and goats, sheep, cattle and fowl living inside.

131
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:33,753
Xenophon doesn't name Derinkuyu.

132
00:09:33,753 --> 00:09:42,927
But he shows that living underground with your animals was already ordinary in Anatolia, twenty-four centuries ago.

133
00:09:42,927 --> 00:09:44,683
Not a lost technology.

134
00:09:44,683 --> 00:09:45,799
A local habit.

135
00:09:46,266 --> 00:09:47,448
Evening is coming.

136
00:09:47,448 --> 00:09:52,700
And as the light fades above, the people below turn to the deepest rooms of all.

137
00:09:53,166 --> 00:09:54,467
Six in the evening.

138
00:09:54,467 --> 00:10:02,819
On the second level there's a large hall with a barrel-vaulted ceiling, unique in the complex, with small rooms beside it.

139
00:10:02,819 --> 00:10:05,899
It's usually described as a religious school.

140
00:10:06,366 --> 00:10:11,257
Below, staircases lead down to a church, cut in the shape of a cross.

141
00:10:11,257 --> 00:10:17,566
The people sheltering here were Christians: Cappadocian Greeks, and later also Armenians.

142
00:10:18,033 --> 00:10:26,561
The chapels, the crosses, the Greek inscriptions, and the great bulk of objects found inside, are Byzantine and medieval.

143
00:10:26,561 --> 00:10:29,733
The city in its present size belongs to them.

144
00:10:30,200 --> 00:10:32,277
This fits the wider region.

145
00:10:32,277 --> 00:10:36,970
The Goreme valley nearby holds hundreds of rock-cut churches.

146
00:10:36,970 --> 00:10:44,433
UNESCO lists Derinkuyu and Kaymakli as part of the same World Heritage site, as places of refuge.

147
00:10:44,899 --> 00:10:51,192
So at six in the evening, while horsemen search the empty village above, the people below pray.

148
00:10:51,192 --> 00:10:52,650
That's the real scene.

149
00:10:52,650 --> 00:10:55,299
And it's harder to watch than any alien.

150
00:10:55,766 --> 00:10:57,536
Now the long night begins.

151
00:10:57,536 --> 00:11:00,735
And with it, the hardest question in this case.

152
00:11:00,735 --> 00:11:02,233
How old is this place?

153
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:03,712
Nine at night.

154
00:11:03,712 --> 00:11:05,593
Here's the honest problem.

155
00:11:05,593 --> 00:11:07,692
You can't carbon-date a hole.

156
00:11:07,692 --> 00:11:09,717
Cutting a room removes rock.

157
00:11:09,717 --> 00:11:11,598
It leaves nothing to test.

158
00:11:11,598 --> 00:11:15,433
Objects found inside only tell you who used it later.

159
00:11:15,899 --> 00:11:25,172
The most common view credits the first chambers to the Phrygians, Iron Age rock carvers, in the eighth or seventh century BC.

160
00:11:25,172 --> 00:11:31,700
Some scholars suggest earlier Hittite beginnings, pointing to a few Hittite-style finds.

161
00:11:32,166 --> 00:11:33,848
Hancock goes much further.

162
00:11:33,848 --> 00:11:46,333
Since the rock can't be dated, he argues, the first digging could be twelve thousand years old: a shelter from the comet impacts he believes struck at the end of the Ice Age, the Younger Dryas.

163
00:11:46,799 --> 00:11:51,309
Supporters link it to a Zoroastrian text, the Vendidad.

164
00:11:51,309 --> 00:12:03,200
The god Ahura Mazda warns King Yima of evil winters and deadly frost, and Yima builds a Vara, an enclosure, to save the seeds of men and animals.

165
00:12:03,666 --> 00:12:07,746
But the Vendidad names no real place and gives no date.

166
00:12:07,746 --> 00:12:11,825
And thousands of years of living always leaves rubbish.

167
00:12:11,825 --> 00:12:15,385
Here, the datable material is Iron Age or later.

168
00:12:15,385 --> 00:12:20,800
No Ice Age layer has ever been found in any Cappadocian underground city.

169
00:12:21,266 --> 00:12:25,478
Hancock also suggested archaeologists were hiding new dates.

170
00:12:25,478 --> 00:12:29,900
Archaeologist Flint Dibble pointed out the dates are published.

171
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:33,200
The signs at the site just hadn't been updated.

172
00:12:33,666 --> 00:12:35,031
So what can we say?

173
00:12:35,031 --> 00:12:37,903
The first cut date is genuinely unknown.

174
00:12:37,903 --> 00:12:44,007
But every piece of evidence we have points to an Iron Age start and a Byzantine peak.

175
00:12:44,007 --> 00:12:47,166
Unknown does not mean twelve thousand years.

176
00:12:47,633 --> 00:12:51,147
And somewhere above, the raiders give up and ride away.

177
00:12:51,147 --> 00:12:52,233
It's almost dawn.

178
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:54,008
Five in the morning.

179
00:12:54,008 --> 00:12:54,662
All clear.

180
00:12:54,662 --> 00:12:57,933
Before the doors roll open, let's score the night.

181
00:12:58,399 --> 00:13:04,747
Inside-only stone doors and crawl-height tunnels: evidence of defence against people.

182
00:13:04,747 --> 00:13:09,228
Staggered air shafts and a well sealed from below: evidence.

183
00:13:09,228 --> 00:13:12,737
Chapels, crosses and Byzantine finds: evidence.

184
00:13:12,737 --> 00:13:17,666
Use as a refuge recorded from Timur to nineteen oh-nine: evidence.

185
00:13:18,133 --> 00:13:23,057
A shelter from attacks from the sky, or from aliens: no evidence.

186
00:13:23,057 --> 00:13:26,769
A twelve-thousand-year comet bunker: no evidence.

187
00:13:26,769 --> 00:13:31,466
The exact first cut, and the full Kaymakli tunnel: still open.

188
00:13:31,933 --> 00:13:32,771
The verdict.

189
00:13:32,771 --> 00:13:39,059
Derinkuyu was dug by people, in soft rock, over many centuries, to hide from other people.

190
00:13:39,059 --> 00:13:52,333
It peaked when Byzantine villages fled Arab raids, and it was still saving lives in nineteen oh-nine, when news of the Adana massacres sent a Cappadocian town underground for several nights.

191
00:13:52,799 --> 00:13:59,296
In nineteen twenty-three, the population exchange sent the Christians of Cappadocia to Greece.

192
00:13:59,296 --> 00:14:06,000
The doors stayed open, the city fell silent, and forty years later a sledgehammer found it again.

193
00:14:06,466 --> 00:14:07,934
So, hiding from what?

194
00:14:07,934 --> 00:14:09,122
Not from the sky.

195
00:14:09,122 --> 00:14:10,100
From the road.

196
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:14,572
From the armies that crossed this land for three thousand years.

197
00:14:14,572 --> 00:14:16,808
And that is not a smaller story.

198
00:14:16,808 --> 00:14:18,066
It's a harder one.

199
00:14:18,533 --> 00:14:21,320
Which hour of the siege surprised you most?

200
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:26,766
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
